afghan officials say the number of  people killed in multiple blasts near a  school in kabul has risen to 50.  the explosions went off in a largely  sheer muslim neighborhood that's faced  attacks by the so-called islamic state  in recent years  no group has so far claimed  responsibility afghanistan's president  is blaming the taliban  but it's denied the attack instead  pointing the finger at  is militants  just hours before there were school  children preparing for the future  now their covered bodies lie on a  hospital floor  it is a site that has become all too  common in afghanistan  multiple blasts went off outside the  syed al shakhtar school in western kabul  as lessons ended for the day many of the  children killed would have been on their  way home  to break the ramadan fast with their  families  i was with my classmate we were leaving  school when suddenly an explosion  happened  my classmate was killed ten minutes  later another explosion and then a third  one  everyone was screaming and there was  blood everywhere i couldn't see anything  clearly  the attack comes just eight days after a  truck bomb  at a university entrance south of kabul  left 27 people dead  from kabul i'm joined by journalist ali  latifi  ali this is the second attack in as many  weeks  and the u.s is slowly pulling out of  afghanistan  is the security situation deteriorating  i don't think it's deteriorating i don't  think it was very good to begin with  these sorts of attacks were taking place  while the us was here  it's certainly not as if the us was  stationed outside girls schools or  any other schools are actively  protecting civilian targets  in afghanistan unfortunately this has  just been the situation  in the country over the last several  years  um and that's just the sad reality of  the country at the moment  ali the target as you say seems to have  been a girl's  school what does that tell us  it look as as you said earlier in the  video package you know just  a week or two prior another group of  students in bogata province also came  under attack um you know it's a way of  attacking the young people of the  country the future of the country i went  to that neighborhood today in kabul  where the attack took place  and i spoke to several families who  lived in the nearby area who lived near  the pool  who said that their children  specifically their daughters were now  like  at night they were crying they were  saying if we go don't send us to school  please don't send us to school if you  send us to school we'll die  going to school will mean death um and  the mothers are now trying to convince  their children to go back to you know  saying  you'll take a break for a while but you  have to go back you have to continue  your education um so this is definitely  you know  having the impact that whoever is behind  it is wanting it to have  which is basically a making people feel  as if they're not secure  or solidifying the fact that they don't  feel secure and  also for the younger generations  especially the youngest ones you know  the mother we were speaking  to she was saying her children were six  and nine years old it's creating a fear  of public institutions  things that are essential like a school  like an education you know uh people in  the area were saying if you look  look at what's being targeted it's  mosques it's schools it's education  centers it's hospitals  um you know these are all basic services  of everyday life  thank you very much for the update  journalist ali latifi in kabul
 

